WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.933
Model monitoring.

2
00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:06.367
A model can be a perfectly healthy service and still be completely wrong.

3
00:00:07.567 --> 00:00:11.100
Monitoring has two layers, and the trap is stopping at the first.

4
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:15.523
Layer one is the serving system: latency, errors, and throughput.

5
00:00:15.523 --> 00:00:17.933
Your existing stack already covers it.

6
00:00:18.233 --> 00:00:24.633
Layer two is the predictions themselves: the output distribution, class balance, and feature statistics.

7
00:00:24.933 --> 00:00:27.406
The left dashboard says the service is up.

8
00:00:27.406 --> 00:00:30.467
Only the right one says the model has lost its mind.

9
00:00:31.667 --> 00:00:35.133
Here is a FastAPI endpoint that logs both views.

10
00:00:35.433 --> 00:00:39.269
We count predictions per class, which gives a rolling class balance.

11
00:00:39.269 --> 00:00:40.567
That is the model view.

12
00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:44.685
We log every prediction with its confidence and model version.

13
00:00:44.685 --> 00:00:48.133
This is raw material for drift detection and retraining.

14
00:00:48.433 --> 00:00:51.067
And we log latency, which is the service view.

15
00:00:52.267 --> 00:00:55.100
Now alert on distributions, not just errors.

16
00:00:55.400 --> 00:01:01.433
If the planet rate jumps from three percent to thirty one, someone should be paged, even though nothing crashed.

17
00:01:02.633 --> 00:01:04.667
Here is how this fails in real life.

18
00:01:04.967 --> 00:01:09.100
A firmware update rescales one telemetry channel by a factor of ten.

19
00:01:09.400 --> 00:01:11.139
The endpoint keeps serving.

20
00:01:11.139 --> 00:01:17.067
Latency is perfect and errors are zero, and the service dashboard stays green for six weeks.

21
00:01:17.367 --> 00:01:20.201
The prediction log would have flagged it in an hour.

22
00:01:20.201 --> 00:01:22.000
That line is your smoke detector.

23
00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.533
The classic mistake is trusting a green service dashboard.

24
00:01:26.833 --> 00:01:30.567
The fix is to also alert on what the model actually predicts.

25
00:01:31.767 --> 00:01:32.494
Let's recap.

26
00:01:32.494 --> 00:01:35.100
Watch both the service and the predictions.

27
00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:37.733
A healthy service can hide a broken model.

28
00:01:38.033 --> 00:01:40.800
Log every prediction, with its model version.

29
00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.482
And alert on distribution changes.

30
00:01:43.482 --> 00:01:45.233
Next up: drift detection.
